WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.717
This briefing is Book 07 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.717 --> 00:00:07.400
PKI and Certificates.

4
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.427
It covers the move from Active Directory
Certificate Services to Microsoft

5
00:00:12.427 --> 00:00:17.062
Cloud PKI, Entra certificate-based
authentication and the Federal PKI.

6
00:00:17.062 --> 00:00:19.075
Here's why it deserves its own book.

7
00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:24.000
Every other migration changes which
system answers the question, who is this?

8
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.513
Certificates are how
that answer gets proved.

9
00:00:26.513 --> 00:00:28.793
So when they're handled
as an afterthought,

10
00:00:28.793 --> 00:00:31.987
things break long after everyone
thought the work was done.

11
00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:35.885
We'll go in four steps: why
certificates outlive every migration,

12
00:00:35.885 --> 00:00:39.709
what the target PKI looks like,
who owns the certificate estate,

13
00:00:39.709 --> 00:00:43.238
and how to move it without
stranding a single certificate.

14
00:00:43.238 --> 00:00:44.990
Let's start with the why.

15
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.928
Certificates outlive migrations because a
certificate isn't a record in a directory.

16
00:00:50.928 --> 00:00:54.052
It's a signed statement that's
already been handed out.

17
00:00:54.052 --> 00:00:57.165
When the directory moves,
the certificate doesn't.

18
00:00:57.165 --> 00:01:01.540
It keeps working until it expires,
and then whatever relied on it stops.

19
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.689
And a lot relies on it: domain
controllers, smart card and PIV logon,

20
00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:12.315
Wi-Fi and VPN, TLS listeners like SQL
Server, code signing and encryption.

21
00:01:12.315 --> 00:01:14.365
That gives the program two clocks.

22
00:01:14.365 --> 00:01:18.008
On the directory's clock,
cutover is declared complete,

23
00:01:18.008 --> 00:01:22.865
and months later the first expiry wave
finds no issuer, far from its cause.

24
00:01:22.865 --> 00:01:26.657
On the certificate's clock,
the replacement issuer is

25
00:01:26.657 --> 00:01:30.277
live before the first expiry,
and AD CS retires last.

26
00:01:30.277 --> 00:01:32.864
The program runs on the
certificate's clock.

27
00:01:32.864 --> 00:01:36.410
And the rules certificates live
under are already changing.

28
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.522
A certificate estate used to be something
you could leave alone between renewals.

29
00:01:41.522 --> 00:01:43.210
Three changes have ended that.

30
00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:47.485
First, public TLS lifetimes are
shrinking on a fixed schedule.

31
00:01:47.485 --> 00:01:51.798
The maximum fell to
200 days in March 2026,

32
00:01:51.798 --> 00:01:57.760
drops to 100 days in March 2027,
and to 47 days in March 2029.

33
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.185
On the book's arithmetic, a thousand
certificates means about seven

34
00:02:02.185 --> 00:02:06.373
renewals every working day today,
and about thirty-one at 47 days.

35
00:02:06.373 --> 00:02:09.210
Second, the rules for
mapping certificates to

36
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.497
accounts changed under
certificates already in use.

37
00:02:12.497 --> 00:02:17.660
Domain controllers moved to full
enforcement in 2025, with no way back.

38
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.272
Third, the algorithms have a horizon.

39
00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:25.002
NIST's draft plan would
deprecate RSA-2048 after

40
00:02:25.002 --> 00:02:30.323
2030 and disallow RSA and
ECDSA signatures after 2035.

41
00:02:30.323 --> 00:02:33.445
Put together, manual
renewal can't keep up,

42
00:02:33.445 --> 00:02:36.660
so the move off AD CS
runs as its own program.

43
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.500
First, what does AD CS hold up?

44
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.708
Before you can move AD CS, you
need to know what it holds up,

45
00:02:43.708 --> 00:02:45.362
and it isn't just one server.

46
00:02:45.362 --> 00:02:49.993
It's a hierarchy of CAs, a set of
forest-wide objects like templates and

47
00:02:49.993 --> 00:02:54.950
the NTAuth store, and Group Policy
autoenrollment on every domain member.

48
00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:58.497
The inventory has four layers:
the CAs, the templates,

49
00:02:58.497 --> 00:03:02.538
the certificates actually
issued, and the relying parties.

50
00:03:02.538 --> 00:03:06.379
The relying parties set the order
of work, because a new issuer is

51
00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:10.512
useful only once everything that
checks its certificates trusts it.

52
00:03:10.512 --> 00:03:14.200
Then every template and use
case gets one of four verdicts.

53
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.688
Migrate it to the cloud.

54
00:03:15.688 --> 00:03:17.817
Bridge it, running both issuers,

55
00:03:17.817 --> 00:03:21.749
where the consumer depends on
AD, like domain controllers.

56
00:03:21.749 --> 00:03:26.138
Retain it where there's no cloud
equivalent, like HSM-held code signing.

57
00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:27.263
Or retire it.

58
00:03:27.263 --> 00:03:30.246
The book also corrects its source here:

59
00:03:30.246 --> 00:03:34.350
internal web and SQL Server
TLS can't move to Cloud PKI.

60
00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.760
And whatever stays has to be hardened.

61
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.761
AD CS keeps issuing trusted
credentials while the migration runs,

62
00:03:41.761 --> 00:03:46.785
so the CA you're leaving has to be the
best-defended system you own until it's gone.

63
00:03:46.785 --> 00:03:49.389
The book treats hardening
as a detect-and-fix

64
00:03:49.389 --> 00:03:51.923
checklist of eight known weakness classes.

65
00:03:51.923 --> 00:03:55.580
Think of things like templates that
let a requester name any subject,

66
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.723
or permissions that let the
wrong people change a template.

67
00:03:58.723 --> 00:04:00.472
The slide lists all eight.

68
00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:05.485
Defender for Identity detects most of
them, as long as its sensor is on the CA.

69
00:04:05.485 --> 00:04:09.210
One class has no automated
check, so it's a manual review.

70
00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.485
The gate is simple.

71
00:04:10.485 --> 00:04:15.701
Every Critical and High finding is fixed
and verified before any workload migrates,

72
00:04:15.701 --> 00:04:19.948
because the recommended cloud issuer
chains to this same hierarchy.

73
00:04:19.948 --> 00:04:23.472
And none of it has evidence
until CA auditing is turned on,

74
00:04:23.472 --> 00:04:25.647
which takes two settings together.

75
00:04:25.647 --> 00:04:28.780
With the old side secured,
what are we moving to?

76
00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:34.055
The target isn't simply
Cloud PKI instead of AD CS.

77
00:04:34.055 --> 00:04:37.155
That's true for devices
and wrong for the estate.

78
00:04:37.155 --> 00:04:40.892
The target is four issuers,
each serving what only it can.

79
00:04:40.892 --> 00:04:45.355
Cloud PKI issues to Intune-managed
devices, and only to them.

80
00:04:45.355 --> 00:04:49.674
A smaller, hardened AD CS
stays for domain controllers,

81
00:04:49.674 --> 00:04:54.568
RADIUS, internal server TLS,
network devices and code signing.

82
00:04:54.568 --> 00:04:59.992
The Federal PKI keeps issuing PIV,
and the target's job is to trust it.

83
00:04:59.992 --> 00:05:03.554
And a public CA serves names
the internet must trust.

84
00:05:03.554 --> 00:05:06.280
One decision ties them together: the root.

85
00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:10.920
For an established hierarchy, the
program recommends bring-your-own-CA,

86
00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:15.167
where the cloud issuing CA is signed
by the existing offline root.

87
00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:16.364
That keeps the chain,

88
00:05:16.364 --> 00:05:20.093
but it makes the root's revocation
list a production dependency.

89
00:05:20.093 --> 00:05:24.838
And check the licensing: the 2026
change that bundles Cloud PKI into

90
00:05:24.838 --> 00:05:29.754
E5 covers commercial customers, so
confirm it for a government tenant.

91
00:05:29.754 --> 00:05:32.780
Four issuers need one
way to keep them renewed.

92
00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.117
Four issuers need one lifecycle platform.

93
00:05:36.117 --> 00:05:40.468
Intune and Cloud PKI renew what
they put on managed devices.

94
00:05:40.468 --> 00:05:45.307
But nothing Microsoft-native does
that for AD CS server certificates,

95
00:05:45.307 --> 00:05:49.729
public TLS, appliances, or the
certificates nobody inventoried.

96
00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:52.993
That's the job of certificate
lifecycle management:

97
00:05:52.993 --> 00:05:57.243
find every certificate, give it an
owner, and renew it automatically.

98
00:05:57.243 --> 00:06:00.642
The program's platform is
CyberArk Certificate Manager.

99
00:06:00.642 --> 00:06:03.354
CyberArk is the program's existing vendor,

100
00:06:03.354 --> 00:06:06.918
so it consolidates there
instead of adding a second one.

101
00:06:06.918 --> 00:06:09.761
The names have shifted over
the last couple of years,

102
00:06:09.761 --> 00:06:11.480
and the notes trace the history.

103
00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.418
Two facts shape the deployment.

104
00:06:13.418 --> 00:06:16.798
There's no FedRAMP listing
for the certificate product,

105
00:06:16.798 --> 00:06:21.868
so the program runs the self-hosted edition
inside its own authorization boundary.

106
00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:25.627
And its AD CS account holds
certificate-manager rights,

107
00:06:25.627 --> 00:06:29.468
so it's scoped and reviewed
like any certificate manager.

108
00:06:29.468 --> 00:06:31.600
Which raises the people question.

109
00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.425
Who owns the certificate estate today?

110
00:06:34.425 --> 00:06:37.143
In most forests, whoever installed the CA.

111
00:06:37.143 --> 00:06:41.381
Domain and enterprise admins are
CA administrators by default,

112
00:06:41.381 --> 00:06:43.300
and nobody ever decided that.

113
00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.463
The book fixes it by separating
deciding from doing.

114
00:06:46.463 --> 00:06:52.161
The sponsor and CIO decide the target
hierarchy, when each piece of AD CS retires,

115
00:06:52.161 --> 00:06:55.787
and who owns renewal for
each class of certificate.

116
00:06:55.787 --> 00:06:58.967
The CISO and ISSO decide issuance policy,

117
00:06:58.967 --> 00:07:02.600
trust anchors and the
minimum binding strength.

118
00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.825
The authorizing official
accepts standing exceptions.

119
00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:09.098
On the doing side, PKI
administrators run the CAs,

120
00:07:09.098 --> 00:07:14.744
the identity team owns the Entra trust
store, endpoint engineering owns the Intune

121
00:07:14.744 --> 00:07:20.063
profiles, and the lifecycle platform
owner runs discovery and server renewal.

122
00:07:20.063 --> 00:07:22.563
The principle is separation of duties.

123
00:07:22.563 --> 00:07:27.688
Whoever can change a CA must never be the
only person who decides what it issues.

124
00:07:27.688 --> 00:07:30.920
Now, how the certificates
actually get delivered.

125
00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:33.095
Start with devices.

126
00:07:33.095 --> 00:07:35.806
Intune delivers certificates three ways,

127
00:07:35.806 --> 00:07:39.370
and each needs the trusted
root profile deployed first.

128
00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:44.202
SCEP from Cloud PKI is the cleanest:
the device makes its own key,

129
00:07:44.202 --> 00:07:47.083
with no on-premises server in the path.

130
00:07:47.083 --> 00:07:51.354
SCEP from AD CS runs through an
NDES server and a connector,

131
00:07:51.354 --> 00:07:53.846
so its permissions are kept tight.

132
00:07:53.846 --> 00:07:57.807
PKCS makes the key on the
connector server and exports it,

133
00:07:57.807 --> 00:08:00.420
which is why the program prefers SCEP.

134
00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.007
The big change is strong mapping,
and it's no longer optional.

135
00:08:04.007 --> 00:08:08.008
Domain controllers have been in
full enforcement since 2025,

136
00:08:08.008 --> 00:08:09.420
and there's no way back.

137
00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:12.188
A certificate used against
a domain controller

138
00:08:12.188 --> 00:08:14.957
without a strong mapping
is simply rejected now.

139
00:08:14.957 --> 00:08:18.086
So the SCEP profiles add
the user's or device's

140
00:08:18.086 --> 00:08:21.807
on-premises security
identifier to the certificate.

141
00:08:21.807 --> 00:08:26.983
Pilot that in a new profile, because editing
an existing one revokes what it issued.

142
00:08:26.983 --> 00:08:28.407
That's the device side.

143
00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:31.750
On the user side, Entra
checks certificates.

144
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:36.050
Entra certificate-based
authentication is a relying party.

145
00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:40.588
It checks certificates and issues
none, so its risk runs the other way:

146
00:08:40.588 --> 00:08:43.375
a certificate that should fail might pass.

147
00:08:43.375 --> 00:08:45.763
Trust and revocation come first.

148
00:08:45.763 --> 00:08:50.006
Upload every CA in the chain,
give each one a revocation list

149
00:08:50.006 --> 00:08:54.250
Entra can reach quickly, and
turn on Require CRL validation.

150
00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:58.775
A CA uploaded without a CRL lets
revoked certificates keep working.

151
00:08:58.775 --> 00:09:02.875
Then binding, meaning how a
certificate is matched to a user.

152
00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:06.975
Some bindings are high affinity,
like issuer and serial number.

153
00:09:06.975 --> 00:09:12.212
Others, like a name or email, are low,
and the old guide got one of those wrong.

154
00:09:12.212 --> 00:09:15.510
A certificate counts as
multifactor only when

155
00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.638
a PIN or biometric protects
it, like a PIV card.

156
00:09:18.638 --> 00:09:21.140
Production requires high affinity,

157
00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:24.726
because CBA is only as strong
as its weakest binding.

158
00:09:24.726 --> 00:09:29.700
But only switch once every user's certificate
IDs are right, or they're locked out.

159
00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:32.870
Next, running both PKIs at once.

160
00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:37.220
For most of the migration,
both PKIs are in production.

161
00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:42.845
Devices carry certificates from both issuers,
and relying parties trust both chains.

162
00:09:42.845 --> 00:09:44.607
Three rules make that safe.

163
00:09:44.607 --> 00:09:46.969
Trust goes up before issuance moves.

164
00:09:46.969 --> 00:09:51.520
Nothing is revoked to force a move;
old certificates leave at renewal.

165
00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:56.164
And every revocation list must be
reachable from inside, from the internet

166
00:09:56.164 --> 00:10:01.182
and from Entra, because most coexistence
outages are really revocation outages.

167
00:10:01.182 --> 00:10:03.541
The migration runs in six gated waves,

168
00:10:03.541 --> 00:10:08.333
about forty-four weeks on the program's
planning figures for a single forest.

169
00:10:08.333 --> 00:10:11.668
It goes from assessment,
through a foundation and pilot,

170
00:10:11.668 --> 00:10:15.293
to scaling across every managed
device, cutting over sign-in,

171
00:10:15.293 --> 00:10:18.483
and finally decommissioning
what's no longer needed.

172
00:10:18.483 --> 00:10:20.708
The slide shows each wave's gate.

173
00:10:20.708 --> 00:10:23.284
Every wave has a rehearsed rollback,

174
00:10:23.284 --> 00:10:27.320
and AD FS stays restorable
for thirty days after cutover.

175
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.270
But the migration ending isn't the end.

176
00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.295
Migration ends on a
date; certificates don't.

177
00:10:34.295 --> 00:10:37.366
With public TLS lifetimes
heading to 47 days,

178
00:10:37.366 --> 00:10:41.683
no certificate class can depend
on someone remembering a date.

179
00:10:41.683 --> 00:10:44.045
Three renewal engines cover the target.

180
00:10:44.045 --> 00:10:46.357
Intune renews managed devices.

181
00:10:46.357 --> 00:10:52.207
AD CS autoenrollment renews domain
controllers and RADIUS while the forest stays.

182
00:10:52.207 --> 00:10:55.798
And CyberArk Certificate
Manager renews servers,

183
00:10:55.798 --> 00:11:00.671
appliances and public TLS, using
ACME where the endpoint supports it.

184
00:11:00.671 --> 00:11:05.700
Monitoring watches for warning signs,
not outages: a certificate with

185
00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:10.383
no renewal path, a stale revocation
list, issuance outside policy.

186
00:11:10.383 --> 00:11:14.408
The checks run at least weekly,
and their history is the evidence.

187
00:11:14.408 --> 00:11:19.570
Retiring an AD CS CA comes last, and
the slide walks through the steps.

188
00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:23.506
The key one is easy to forget:
keep its revocation endpoints

189
00:11:23.506 --> 00:11:27.120
serving until the last
certificate it issued expires.

190
00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.258
Under bring-your-own-CA, the root
that signed the cloud issuer stays.

191
00:11:31.258 --> 00:11:33.190
So, what do we need decided?

192
00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:35.540
Here are the four decisions.

193
00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:39.997
First, confirm the root: bring-your-own-CA,
signed by the offline root,

194
00:11:39.997 --> 00:11:43.077
once the hierarchy passes
the hardening gate.

195
00:11:43.077 --> 00:11:47.066
Second, settle the Cloud PKI
licensing for the government tenant,

196
00:11:47.066 --> 00:11:50.315
rather than assuming the
commercial change applies.

197
00:11:50.315 --> 00:11:54.249
Third, stand up the self-hosted
CyberArk Certificate Manager

198
00:11:54.249 --> 00:11:58.415
inside the authorization boundary
as the single lifecycle tool.

199
00:11:58.415 --> 00:12:03.500
And fourth, name the owners: issuance
policy and trust anchors with the CISO,

200
00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:07.052
and one renewal owner for
every class of certificate.

201
00:12:07.052 --> 00:12:09.753
The next step is wave one, Assess.

202
00:12:09.753 --> 00:12:14.714
That's four weeks to inventory every
CA and template, close the Critical

203
00:12:14.714 --> 00:12:19.677
and High findings, classify every use
case, and list every relying party.

204
00:12:19.677 --> 00:12:21.848
Run certificates on their own clock,

205
00:12:21.848 --> 00:12:25.103
and the old CAs can retire
without stranding anything.

206
00:12:25.103 --> 00:12:28.246
Book 07 has the detail,
every claim footnoted,

207
00:12:28.246 --> 00:12:32.664
with deployment checklists and
a template-by-template matrix.

208
00:12:32.664 --> 00:12:33.480
Thank you.
